course: dictionary
lesson: 8298
title: Riverbank
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A riverbank is the land along the side of a river. Every river has two banks, one on each side, so you can stand on one riverbank and look across at the other.
explain: «Scarlet macaws ... eat clay from riverbanks, which scientists think gives them scarce salt.» The clue is that birds are eating clay from the land beside the river, so a riverbank is the land where the river's edge meets the land.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. "Scarlet macaws eat clay from the land along the side of a river" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A riverbank is the land along the side of a river.
fact: Every river has two banks, one on each side.
fact: Animals such as otters, water voles and kingfishers live on riverbanks.
fact: On a bend, the water wears away the outer bank. This wearing away is called erosion.
fact: Riverbanks can be slippery or crumble, so stay back from the edge.
exercise: Every river has two what, one on each side? | *banks | roads | fish | clouds
exercise: What is a riverbank? | A bridge over a river | *The land along the side of a river | The bottom of the sea | A tall tree in a forest
exercise: Riverbanks can be slippery or crumble. Why should you stay back from the edge? | Because there are no animals there | Because riverbanks are warm and soft | Because the water is always too hot | *Because the ground might crumble and you could fall in
test: «Scarlet macaws ... eat clay from riverbanks.» What do the macaws eat from the riverbanks? | Fish | Sand | Leaves | *Clay
test: Which animal from the lessons lives on riverbanks? | A snake | A camel | A penguin | *A kingfisher
exam: What is the word for water wearing away the outer bank on a bend? | Freezing | Rain | Sunshine | *Erosion
